* Appending to a slice

It is common to append new elements to a slice, and so ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000001⟫ provides a built-in
⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000002⟫append⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000003⟫ function. The [[⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000004⟫][documentation]]
of the built-in package describes ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000005⟫append⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000006⟫.

⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000007⟫

The first parameter ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000008⟫s⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000009⟫ of ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000010⟫append⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000011⟫ is a slice of type ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000012⟫T⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000013⟫, and the rest are
⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000014⟫T⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000015⟫ values to append to the slice.

The resulting value of ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000016⟫append⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000017⟫ is a slice containing all the elements of the
original slice plus the provided values.

If the backing array of ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000018⟫s⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000019⟫ is too small to fit all the given values a bigger
array will be allocated. The returned slice will point to the newly allocated
array.

(To learn more about slices, read the [[⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000020⟫][Slices: usage and internals]] article.)

